Programming language logos in README.md
=======================================

This file sits beside the images it describes, and not in the repository's root
NOTICE, because no package ships these files. The four packaged bindings each
carry a copy of that NOTICE (Apache 2.0 section 4(a)), and a wheel or an npm
tarball claiming to contain a share-alike licensed image it does not contain is
a claim a license scanner reads and a reader cannot check.

`images/` holds the logos of the five languages this project is built from and
spoken from, shown in README.md to identify them. Each is used nominatively, to
name the language it stands for, and no endorsement by any of these projects is
claimed or implied. Every mark remains the property of its owner.

Each file was resized and centered on a common canvas so the five read at one
size. None was recolored, cropped, redrawn or stretched, with one stated
exception: the Rust and Julia marks are near-black and would be invisible on a
dark page background, so each also has a light-on-dark variant, served by a
`prefers-color-scheme` switch. In the Rust variant the black is white. In the
Julia variant only the near-black wordmark is white and the four colored dots
are untouched.

    R           The R logo, (C) 2016 The R Foundation, licensed CC BY-SA 4.0.
                https://www.r-project.org/logo/
    Python      The Python logo, a trademark of the Python Software Foundation.
    Julia       The Julia logo, from the Julia programming language project.
    Rust        The Rust logo, a trademark of the Rust Foundation.
    JavaScript  The "JS" mark, an unofficial community logo rather than an
                official one, no official JavaScript logo existing.
                "JavaScript" is a trademark of Oracle Corporation.

The R logo is share-alike licensed, which is why it appears in README.md, under
Apache 2.0, and not in the book, whose prose is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0: a
NonCommercial restriction cannot be added on top of CC BY-SA.
